messages[14]{user,text}:
  user_2,[00:00] Hi Pooja, this is Anita from Trust Wealth Advisors at DCoders. Can you hear me okay?
  user_1,Yeah, I can hear you. Good morning.
  user_2,[00:15] Great, thanks. I’m calling about your health policy—policy number HP-4521. I see it’s set to expire on 30/09/2026.
  user_1,Oh—uhh, really? I kinda forgot about that. [sighs]
  user_2,[00:35] No worries. Once it expires you won’t have coverage for any new claims, and you’d need to either renew or pick a new plan.
  user_1,Umm, what are my options if I want to stay covered? Do I have to pay the same premium?
  user_2,[01:00] We have a few choices. You can renew the same plan at the current rate, which has gone up about 8% this year, or we can look at a lower‑premium alternative that has a higher deductible.
  user_1,[01:20] I see… I’m not sure I want to keep paying that much. Maybe a lower‑premium option?
  user_2,[01:35] Sure, I can pull up a couple of options. One is a basic health cover with a ₹10,000 deductible, premium would be around ₹4,200 per month. The other is a short‑term extension for three months at the current rate, just to give you more time.
  user_1,[02:00] Hmm, three months sounds okay… but I need to think about it. Can you send me the details by email?
  user_2,[02:15] Absolutely. I’ll email you the comparison sheet and the renewal form. Just to confirm, the email we have on file is pooja.bhatia@example.com, right?
  user_1,Yeah, that’s correct. Thanks.
  user_2,[02:40] Great, I’ll send that right away. If you have any questions after reviewing, feel free to call back. Anything else I can help you with right now?
  user_1,No, that’s all. Thanks for the call. Bye.